---
/**
 * 1910 beside today. Two photographs, one argument: the city has been doing
 * this for a hundred and twenty years, and the building it opened in 2021 is
 * the same commitment in a different material.
 *
 * This is the one place the grid is allowed to break, and only the photographs
 * are allowed to break it.
 */
---

<section class="section heritage" id="heritage">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="head reveal">
      <span class="figno">Fig. 03</span>
      <h2>A hundred and twenty years of the same idea</h2>
    </header>

    <div class="pair">
      <figure class="then reveal">
        <picture>
          <source type="image/webp" srcset="/images/carnegie-postcard-900.webp 900w, /images/carnegie-postcard-1400.webp 1400w" sizes="(min-width: 900px) 46vw, 92vw" />
          <img src="/images/carnegie-postcard-900.jpg" alt="A printed view of the Carnegie Public Library in Riverside, California." width="1400" height="880" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </picture>
        <figcaption><span class="figno">Then</span> The Carnegie library, paid for by a steel fortune and a city that wanted one.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="now reveal">
        <picture>
          <source type="image/webp" srcset="/images/main-library-today-605.webp 605w, /images/main-library-today-1100.webp 1100w" sizes="(min-width: 900px) 38vw, 92vw" />
          <img src="/images/main-library-today-605.jpg" alt="The Riverside Main Library at 3900 Mission Inn Avenue, a cantilevered stone building above a red street-level volume at dusk." width="605" height="680" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </picture>
        <figcaption><span class="figno">Now</span> The Main Library, 3900 Mission Inn Avenue. Opened 2021.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="note reveal">
        <p class="prose">
          Andrew Carnegie funded sixteen hundred libraries on one condition: that the town pay to
          keep them running, forever. Riverside said yes. Everything this foundation does is the
          modern half of that bargain, which never actually ends.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .heritage {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .head {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    margin-bottom: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.2rem);
  }
  .head h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4.4vw, 3.2rem);
    max-width: 20ch;
  }

  .pair {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(1.6rem, 4vw, 2.6rem);
  }
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .pair {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.12fr) minmax(0, 0.88fr);
      align-items: start;
    }
    /* The modern plate steps down and overlaps the baseline of the archival
       one: the only break in the grid on the page. */
    .now {
      margin-block-start: 4.5rem;
    }
    .note {
      grid-column: 1;
      max-width: 44ch;
    }
  }
  figure {
    margin: 0;
  }
  figure img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    box-shadow: 0 24px 60px rgba(13, 34, 51, 0.18);
  }
  figcaption {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.25rem;
    margin-top: 0.85rem;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    max-width: 38ch;
  }
</style>
